<& /page/page_title.mas, title=>"基因組：<i>Solanum pennellii</i>(Genome: <i>Solanum pennellii</i>)" &>

<br>
<p>
  <a href="/chado/organism.pl?organism_id=19"><i>Solanum
pennellii</i></a> 是一種與栽培番茄 <a href="/chado/organism.pl?organism_id=1"><i>Solanum
  lycopersicum</i></a> 親緣關係相對較遠的野生番茄。它由馬克斯普朗克分子植物生理學研究所 (MPI Golm) 和亞琛工業大學的一組科學家及其合作者在 Alistair Fernie 和 Bj&ouml;rn Usadel 的指導下完成定序。(is a wild tomato that is relatively distant from the domesticated <i>Solanum
  lycopersicum</i>.  It was sequenced by a group of scientists at
  MPI Golm and the Univeristy of Aachen and collaborators, under the direction of Alistair Fernie and Bj&ouml;rn Usadel.)
<br>
  它已被廣泛用於構建<a href="/cview/map.pl?map_id=9&show_offsets=1&show_ruler=1">定位群體(mapping populations)</a>和<a href="/cview/map.pl?map_id=il6&show_offsets=1&show_ruler=1">自交系(inbred lines)</a>。(It has been widely used for the construction of mapping populations and inbred lines.)
</p>

<br>

<p>
  <strong>請引用 <i>Solanum pennellii</i> 基因組如下：(Please, cite <i>Solanum pennellii</i> genome as:)</strong><br>
  Bolger, A., Scossa, F., Bolger, M. E., Lanz, C., Maumus, F., Tohge, T., ... & Fernie A.R. (2014). <a href="http://www.nature.com/ng/journal/v46/n9/full/ng.3046.html">The genome of the stress-tolerant wild tomato species Solanum pennellii.</a> Nature Genetics 46, 1034–1038
</p>

<br>
<p><strong>摘要(Abstract)</strong><br><i>Solanum pennellii</i> 是南美洲安地斯地區特有的野生番茄物種，在該地區已演化出在乾旱生境中繁衍生息的能力。由於其極端的抗逆性和不尋常的形態，它是栽培番茄 <i>Solanum lycopersicum</i> 的重要種質供體。將 <i>S. lycopersicum</i> 的大基因組區域替換為來自 <i>S. pennellii</i> 的相應片段的滲入系 (IL) 可以顯示出顯著優異的農藝性狀表現。在此，我們描述了 IL 群體親本的高質量基因組組裝。透過將 <i>S. pennellii</i> 基因組錨定到遺傳圖譜上，我們定義了抗逆候選基因，並提供了轉座元件在這些性狀演化中發揮作用的證據。我們的工作為進一步改進番茄以及破譯可利用 <i>S. pennellii</i> 種質改進的眾多其他農藝性狀的機制鋪平了道路。(<i>Solanum pennellii</i> is a wild tomato species endemic to Andean regions in South America, where it has evolved to thrive in arid habitats. Because of its extreme stress tolerance and unusual morphology, it is an important donor of germplasm for the cultivated tomato <i>Solanum lycopersicum</i>. Introgression lines (ILs) in which large genomic regions of <i>S. lycopersicum</i> are replaced with the corresponding segments from <i>S. pennellii</i> can show remarkably superior agronomic performance. Here we describe a high-quality genome assembly of the parents of the IL population. By anchoring the <i>S. pennellii</i> genome to the genetic map, we define candidate genes for stress tolerance and provide evidence that transposable elements had a role in the evolution of these traits. Our work paves a path toward further tomato improvement and for deciphering the mechanisms underlying the myriad other agronomic traits that can be improved with <i>S. pennellii</i> germplasm.)</p>

<br>
